{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "gcp-global-multiregion-relational-app-v1",
  "title": "Global application with regional front ends and a single-region Azure SQL writer",
  "subtitle": "Global ingress routes to regional application tiers, while writes go to a single Azure SQL primary and a secondary receives asynchronous geo-replication. Synthetic Azure reference; no deployment is im",
  "description": "### Problem\nA global audience needs regional application entry points and a recovery design, but the Azure target must not imply the source database's globally synchronous relational semantics.\n\n### Flow and Azure mapping\nAzure Front Door routes users to regional web and application tiers. Route all writes to one Azure SQL primary region. If local reads in a secondary region are needed, use a configured geo-secondary only for workloads that tolerate replication lag; otherwise route reads to the primary. Azure SQL geo-replication is asynchronous, so the secondary is not a globally consistent writable peer. On a regional failure, an operator or tested recovery workflow promotes the secondary and redirects writes according to the chosen recovery process.\n\n### Trade-offs and assumptions\nThe Google source uses regional application tiers with multi-region Spanner, which provides a capability this Azure SQL topology does not preserve. This adaptation **loses synchronous cross-region strong relational consistency and active-active multi-region writes**: secondary reads can be stale, and recent writes may not have replicated at failover. Azure SQL is not a Spanner equivalent. If the workload requires globally consistent relational transactions, this candidate is not a fit; evaluate a different data model or managed database design and document its actual guarantees. No RTO, RPO, latency, or zero-data-loss claim is made.\n\nSource: [Google Cloud Architecture Center](https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/global-deployment-compute-engine-spanner).\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- Origin provider: Google Cloud\n- Target: Azure conceptual adaptation\n- Primary source: [Global deployment with Compute Engine and Spanner](https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/global-deployment-compute-engine-spanner)\n- Reviewed: 2026-11-06\n\n## Supplementary context\n\n- [Google Cloud global deployment archetype](https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/deployment-archetypes/global) — Reviewed: 2026-11-06; Supplementary provider context; not a copied diagram or implementation.\n- [Uncomplicating the complex: How Spanner simplifies microservices-based architectures](https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/databases/why-spanner-is-a-good-fit-for-microservices-based-applications) — Reviewed: 2026-11-06; Provides Google-side context on Spanner's relational and replication properties and trade-offs, making the non-equivalence of the Azure SQL adaptation explicit.\n\n## Catalog status\n\nThis is an original, editable Azure conceptual adaptation. It is synthetic reference material, not a deployed system, certified vendor architecture, or automatic deployment plan.\n\n## Mapping caveats\n\n- This Azure target does not preserve Spanner's globally synchronous relational consistency or multi-region write behavior. Secondary reads can be stale, and failover can have nonzero data loss; verify current Azure SQL geo-replication behavior and workload requirements.",
